Subject: [PATCH 4/7] feat(floor-studio): lay a real floor into a real
 photograph, with no model in sight
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

FS-04 of the Floor Studio series.

No pixel of wood this renderer draws comes from a generative model. Every
board is synthesised from the catalogue record: the species' two pigments,
the finish's tint and sheen, the pattern's geometry, the chosen board
width. A diffusion model asked for "white oak herringbone" returns a
beautiful floor that exists nowhere, and the homeowner who falls for it
cannot buy it. That is not a rendering bug, it is a fraud with a nice
palette.

THREE THINGS MAKE IT READ AS A ROOM, AND ALL THREE ARE ARITHMETIC

Perspective: solveHomography() is an exact projective map from the four
corners the visitor placed, not the affine approximation that makes cheap
visualisers look like a sticker. A test asserts that equal steps in plan
space produce unequal steps in the image — the boards converge because the
maths makes them converge.

The room's own light: every painted pixel keeps the LUMINANCE RATIO of the
pixel it replaces against the floor region's mean. A patch in shadow stays
in shadow. Nothing about the lighting is invented because all of it is
retained, and a test proves the ordering of brightness survives.

What is standing on the floor: a rug, a sofa leg, a plant pot inside the
quad keeps its own pixels, decided by chroma distance from the floor's own
mean. The test paints a blue rug inside the region and asserts it comes
back untouched. Leaving a rough edge looks like a rough edge; painting oak
across the cat looks like a toy.

HERRINGBONE IN O(1)

With boards L cells long and one wide, herringbone reduces to one integer
test: d = (i - j) mod 2L, board runs along X when d < L. That identity is
derived from the lattice the pattern actually has — generators (L+1, 1-L)
and (1, 1) — and the test asserts every board owns exactly L cells with no
gaps and no overlaps.

BOARD SCALE IS AN ASSUMPTION AND SAYS SO

A photograph cannot give a real-world size (room.ts). The visible plane is
treated as a square of the area the visitor typed, which sets how large
the boards look and nothing else — not the price, ever — and the visitor
gets a nudge for rooms that are long and narrow.

render.ts is pure and tested over RGBA in node; render-canvas.ts is the
thin browser edge that decodes, paints and exports. The 1400px downscale
there is the whole performance story: a 12-megapixel phone photo would
freeze the device this feature is mostly used on.

24 tests.

